ture as true.\n\nThe bound of 19n is not tight and it is possible that ssp⁡(𝐺) ≤(1+ 𝑜⁡(1)) ⁢𝑛 for every connected n-vertex graph G,^{1} where the connectivity condition is necessary, as the graph consisting of 𝑛/4 disjoint copies of 𝐾4 requires 5⁢𝑛/4 paths to be strongly separated.\n\n## Discrete Mathematics\n\nDate: September 2026\n\nArticle: 115100\n\nVolume: Volume 349, Issue 9\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\nNote\n\nSeparating path systems for cubic graphs and for complete bipartite graphs☆\n\nAuthor links open overlay panelCristina G. Fernandes ^{c}, Carlos Hoppen ^{a}, George Kontogeorgiou ^{b}, Guilherme Oliveira Mota ^{c}, Danni Peng ^{d}\n\n\n10.1016/j.disc.2026.115100\n\nAccess through your organizationPurchase PDF\n\nMore actions\n\n  * Article preview\n  * Recommended articles\n\n## Abstract\n\nA strongly separating path system in a graph G is a collection 𝒫 of paths in G such that, for every two edges e and f of G, there is a path in 𝒫 with e but not f, and vice-versa. The minimum size of such a system is the so called strong separation number of G. We prove that the strong separation number of every 2-degenerate graph on n vertices is at most n. Using this, we also provide upper bounds for the strong separation number of subcubic graphs, planar graphs, and planar bipartite graphs. On the other hand, we prove that the strong separation number of a complete bipartite graph 𝐾𝑎,𝑏 is at least b if 𝑎< 𝑏/2 and at least (√6⁢(𝑏/2)+4− 2)⁢𝑎 if 𝑏/2 ≤𝑎 ≤𝑏, and we provide a construction that attains the former bound.\n\n## Keywords\n\nSeparating path systems\n\n;\n\n2-Degenerate graphs\n\n;\n\nCubic graphs\n\n;\n\nPlanar graphs\n\n;\n\nBipartite graphs\n\n\n## Introduction\n\nGiven a collection 𝒫 of paths in a graph G, we say that two edges 𝑒,𝑓 in G are separated by 𝒫 if there are two paths 𝑃𝑒 and 𝑃𝑓 in 𝒫 such that 𝑃𝑒 contains e but avoids f, and 𝑃𝑓 contains f but avoids e. We say that 𝒫 is a strongly separating path system of G if 𝒫 separates every pair of edges in G. The minimum size of a strongly separating path system of G is denoted by ssp⁡(𝐺).\n\nBalogh, Csaba, Martin, and Pluhár [1] conjectured that ssp⁡(𝐺)= O⁡(𝑛) for every n-vertex graph G, and succeeded in establishing an upper bound of O⁡(𝑛⁢log⁡𝑛) on ssp⁡(𝐺). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) On list coloring with separation of the complete graph and set system intersections (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/391386504_On_list_coloring_with_separation_of_the_complete_graph_and_set_system_intersections)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Electronic Journal of Graph Theory and Applications 13 (1) (2025), 57–74 ... separation number of the complete graph K n for some values of a, b and n, and prove bounds for\n\n# On list coloring with separation of the complete graph and set system intersections\n\n  * April 2025\n  * Electronic Journal of Graph Theory and Applications 13(1):57\n\nDOI:10.5614/ejgta.2025.13.1.5\n\nAuthors:\n\nJean-Christophe Godin\n\nRémi Grisot\n\n  * Egerie\n\nOlivier Togni\n\n  * Université Bourgogne Europe\n\nImage\n\nDownload full-text PDFRead full-text\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe consider the following list coloring with separation problem: Given a graph G and integers a, b,find the largest integer c such that for any list assignment L of G with |L(v)| = a for any vertex vand |L(u) ∩ L(v)| ≤ c for any edge uv of G, there exists an assignment φ of sets of integers to thevertices of G such that φ(u) ⊂ L(u) and |φ(v)| = b for any vertex u and φ(u) ∩ φ(v) = ∅ for anyedge uv. Such a value of c is called the separation number of (G, a, b). Using a special partitionof a set of lists for which we obtain an improved version of Poincar ́e’s crible, we determine theseparation number of the complete graph Kn for some values of a, b and n, and prove bounds forthe remaining values.\n\nand |L(u)∩L(v)| ≤ c for any edge uv of G, there exists an assignment φ of sets of integers to the\n\nvertices of G such that φ(u)⊂L(u)and |φ(v)|=b for any vertex u and φ(u)∩φ(v) = ∅for any\n\nedge uv. Such a value of c is called the separation number of (G, a, b). Using a special partition\n\nof a set of lists for which we obtain an improved version of Poincar´\n\ne’s crible, we determine the\n\nseparation number of the complete graph K n for some values of a, b and n, and prove bounds for\n\nthe remaining values.\n\nKeywords: graph, coloring, choosability, separation, set system\n\nMathematics Subject Classiﬁcation : 05C15\n\nDOI: 10.5614/ejgta.2025.13.1.5\n\n1. Introduction\n\nLet a, b, c be integers and let G be a graph. A a-list assignment L of G is a function which\n\nassociates to each vertex a set of a integers. The list assignment L is c-separating if for any\n\nuv ∈E(G),|L(u)∩L(v)| ≤ c. The graph G is (a, b, c)-choosable if for any c-separating a-list\n\nassignment L, there exists an (L, b)-coloring of G, i.e. a coloring function φ on the vertices of G\n\nReceived: 20 July 2020, Revised: 2 February 2025, Accepted: 19 February 2025.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSeparating Path Systems in Complete Graphs - Fernandes - 2025 - Random Structures & Algorithms - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/rsa.70006)\nciteturn3search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Pluhr, “On the Path Separation Number of Graphs,” Discrete Applied Mathematics 213 (2016): 26–33, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.dam.2016.05.022. ... Wickes, “Separating Path Systems for the Complete Graph,” Discrete Mathematics 347, no. 3 (2024): 113784, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.disc.2023.113784. ... (2025), Separating Path Systems in Complete Graphs.\n\nVolume 66, Issue 3 e70006\n\nRESEARCH ARTICLE\n\n# Separating Path Systems in Complete Graphs\n\nCristina G. Fernandes, \n\nCristina G. Fernandes\n\nInstituto de Matemática e Estatística, Universidade de São Paulo, São Paulo, Brazil\n\nSearch f