(v \\setminus \\mathord {\\diamond }\\) is the concatenation of \\(w^\\frac{a^{n-d}}{n}\\) for the words \\(w \\in \\left\\{ 0,1,\\ldots , k-1 \\right\\} ^{n-d}\\) in lexicographic order. ... Hurlbert G.: On universal cycles for \\(k\\)-subsets of an \\(n\\)-set.\n\n# The existence and structure of universal partial cycles\n\n  * Open access\n  * Published: 04 July 2026\n\n  * Volume 94, article number 152 (2026)\n  * Cite this article\n\nYou have full access to this open access article\n\nDownload PDF\n\nSave article\n\nView saved research\n\nDesigns, Codes and Cryptography Aims and scope Submit manuscript\n\nThe existence and structure of universal partial cycles\n\nDownload PDF\n\n  * Dylan Fillmore^{1},\n  * Bennet Goeckner^{2},\n  * Rachel Kirsch^{3},\n  * Kirin Martin^{4} &\n  * …\n  * Daniel McGinnis^{5} \nShow authors\n\n  * 456 Accesses\n\n  * Explore all metrics\n\n## Abstract\n\nA universal partial cycle (or upcycle) for \\(\\mathcal {A}^n\\) is a cyclic sequence that covers each word of length n over the alphabet \\(\\mathcal {A}\\) exactly once—like a De Bruijn cycle, except that we also allow a wildcard symbol \\(\\mathord {\\diamond }\\) that can represent any letter of \\(\\mathcal {A}\\). Universal cycles have been studied for many families, including subsets [22], permutations [26], and arrays [23]; we point the reader to [9] for an overview. The most well-known examples of universal cycles are De Bruijn cycles, which are universal cycles for words.\n\nFor a set \\(\\mathcal {A}\\) and positive integer n, let \\(\\mathcal {A}^n\\) denote the set of words of length n with symbols in \\(\\mathcal {A}\\). A De Bruijn cycle for \\(\\mathcal {A}^n\\) is a cyclic sequence of symbols \\((u_1\\cdots u_{N})\\) such that for every \\(w\\in \\mathcal {A}^n\\) there is precisely one index i for which \\(u_iu_{i+1}\\cdots u_{i+n-1}=w\\). Here indices are taken modulo N, the length of the De Bruijn cycle, which is always \\(a^n\\). For example, (00010111) is a De Bruijn cycle for \\(\\left\\{ 0,1 \\right\\} ^3\\) because its consecutive substrings of length 3—000, 001, 010, 101, 011, 111, 110, and 100—cover each binary word of length 3 exactly once. De Bruijn cycles exist for every alphabet size a and every subword length n.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Avoidability beyond paths (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/363053144_Avoidability_beyond_paths)\nciteturn3search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.2 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; Recall that Conjecture 1.9 states that the two-rooted graph T 3(1,0) is inherent. ... in a graph G is closable if there exists a component C of the graph G−N[V(H)] such that each s 0\ntwo conjectures related to the general case.\n\nConjecture 6.1. A two-rooted graph (H, s, t)is not inherent if s and t are not in the same com-\n\nponent of H.\n\nConjecture 6.2. If (H+H 0, s, t)is inherent and s, t are in H, then (H, s, t)is also inherent.\n\nRecall that Conjecture 1.9 states that the two-rooted graph T 3(1,0) is inherent. More generally,\n\nwe ask the following.\n\nQuestion 6.3. Which two-rooted combs of type III are inherent?\n\nThe following much more general questions are also still open.\n\nQuestion 6.4. Is the problem of recognizing if a given two-rooted graph is inherent (resp. PE-\n\ninherent) decidable? If so, is it solvable in polynomial time?\n\nIn conclusion, we mention some possible generalizations of the considered concepts from two-\n\nrooted graphs to k-rooted graphs, that is, graphs H with ordered k-tuples of vertices, for any integer\n\nk≥2.\n\nGiven a graph G, a k-rooted graph ( ˆ\n\nH, ˆ s 1,...,ˆ s k), and a copy (H, s 1, . . . , s k) of it in G, an\n\nextension of (H, s 1, . . . , s k)in G is any k-rooted graph (H 0, s 0\n\n1, . . . , s 0\n\nk) such that H 0 is an induced\n\nsubgraph of G obtained from H by adding to it k pendant edges s 1 s 0\n\n1, . . . , s k s 0\n\nk. More precisely,\n\nV(H 0) = V(H)∪ {s