al. In this paper we prove that s-overlap\ncycles for k-permutations of [n] exist for all k < n.\n1\nIntroduction\narXiv:1301.1270v2  [math.CO]  20 Sep 2013\nMany applications require a set of combinatorial objects to be ordered in a speciﬁc manner.\nOne such ordering is an s-overlap cycle, or s-ocycle. An s-ocycle is an ordering of a set of\nobjects C, each of which has size n and is represented as a string. The ordering requires that\nobject b = b1b2 . . . bn follow object a = a1a2 . . . an only if an−s+1an−s+2 . . . an = b1b2 . . . bs.\nOcycles were introduced by Godbole, Knisley, and Norwood in 2010 [3].\nFor the reader familiar with universal cycles, we note that an (n−1)-ocycle is a universal\ncycle.\nUniversal cycles, or ucycles, were introduced in 1992 by Chung, Diaconis, and\nGraham [1], and have been studied extensively ever since, for example over block designs\n[2].\nUniversal cycles for permutations have long been an interesting research problem.\nWhile ucycles using the standard permutation representation are impossible, it has been\nshown that when n + 1 symbols are used to represent permutations of [n], ucycles are\npossible [5, 8]. Another alternative is to consider k-permutations, as done by Jackson [7].\nJackson proved the existence of ucycles for k-permutations of [n] with 3 ≤k < n. As a\nnatural extension to the problem of ﬁnding ucycles for permutations, we consider ocycles.\n∗vhoran@asu.edu\n†hurlbert@asu.edu\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKolKom 2026 (https://kolkom26.zib.de/)\nciteturn3search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; A de Bruijn sequence packs all $n$-bit binary words into a cycle of length $2^n$. ... Here we consider the natural analogue for permutations using their shorthand representation (i.e., each permutation's final redundant value is omitted from the window). ... Hurlbert. ... “Shorthand Universal Tori for Permutations: Existence, Symmetry, and Generation of Twori.” 